  "worked_solution": "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{3}{10}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{(1\\times 10)+3}{10}=\\frac{13}{10}\n$$\n\nAlso, we write $-\\frac{1}{5}$ as $-\\frac{2}{10}$ and then we simply subtract the numerators. This gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{10}-\\frac{13}{10}=\\frac{-2-13}{10}.\n$$\n\nNow, we work out $-2-13$ using a number line:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14148354/assets/651e46f49551c462bbf15db5e1fc554aa0ff81d7f81fc64286f15c226be69800.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{-2-13}{10}=\\frac{-15}{10}=-\\frac{15}{10}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simplify:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{15}{10}=-\\frac{15÷5}{10÷5}=-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{2}=-1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$",
